  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⚜️ Guild Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Adventurers' companies, thieves' guilds, mage circles, merchant houses and craft guilds."
    },
    "intro": "A guild tells you what it does by its name, and how it wants to be seen: a thieves' guild called <em>the Velvet Hand</em> is selling discretion, a merchants' <em>Honest Ledger Consortium</em> is protesting too much. This **guild name generator** has names for five kinds of guild, each in its own style.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Real guilds",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Medieval guilds</strong> controlled a trade in a town: who could practise it, at what price and to what standard. A craftsman went from apprentice to journeyman to master.</li><li><strong>The masterpiece</strong> was originally the piece of work a journeyman submitted to the guild to be made a master.</li><li><strong>London's livery companies</strong> are guilds that survive today, many with grand names like the Worshipful Company of Goldsmiths or of Clockmakers. That's the style the craft guild names here borrow.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Five kinds of guild",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Adventurers' companies</strong>: named like mercenary bands, after an emblem or a place. <em>The Gilded Lanterns</em>, <em>Ravenscar Company</em>.</li><li><strong>Thieves' guilds</strong>: polite names for impolite work. <em>The Velvet Hand</em>, <em>Brotherhood of the Open Window</em>. In Terry Pratchett's Ankh-Morpork the Thieves' Guild is perfectly legal and issues receipts.</li><li><strong>Mages</strong>: circles, conclaves and orders, named after a symbol. <em>The Conclave of the Burning Key</em>.</li><li><strong>Merchants</strong>: companies named after trade routes or goods. <em>The Saltroad Company</em>, <em>Fellowship of Amber Merchants</em>.</li><li><strong>Craft guilds</strong>: the honest trades. <em>The Worshipful Company of Cartographers</em>, <em>the Patient Loom Guild</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using a guild in your game",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Give it a hall and a rule.</strong> Every guild has a building and at least one rule outsiders find strange.</li><li><strong>Give it a rival.</strong> The mages' circle and the alchemists' guild arguing over who owns fire is a session in itself.</li><li><strong>Online games</strong>: for an MMO guild or a team, the <a href=\"clan-name-generator.html\">Clan Name Generator</a> has gaming names with tags.</li></ul><p>Running a guild on a Minecraft server? The <a href=\"minecraft-clan-name-generator.html\">Minecraft Clan Name Generator</a> makes faction, clan and team names.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is a good name for a thieves' guild?",
        "answer": "Something that sounds respectable, or at least discreet: the Velvet Hand, the Quiet Market, the Brotherhood of the Locked Door. Choose \"Thieves' guild\" for more."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between a guild and a clan?",
        "answer": "A guild is joined for a trade or purpose; a clan is a family or people. In online games the words are often used for the same thing."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D or my book?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site."
      }
    ]
  }
